course: biology
lesson: 178
title: Mangroves: trees in salt water, mudskippers, archerfish and swimming tigers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most trees die if you pour salty sea water on their roots, but mangroves grow with their feet in it, along warm tropical coasts where the tide comes in and out twice a day.
explain: They stand on stilt roots like a table on long legs, and some poke little pencil-like breathing roots up out of the mud, because the sticky mud below has almost no air.
explain: Among those roots live fish that walk on mud, fish that shoot down insects with a squirt of water, crabs waving one giant claw, and in the biggest mangrove forest of all, tigers that swim from island to island.
explain: Mangroves are also a nursery for young fish and a wall against storms: their tangled roots break the waves and hold the soil, like a sponge and a fence in one.
fact: Mangroves are trees that grow in salty water along tropical coasts, and some push salt out through their leaves, where you can see salt crystals.
fact: Stilt roots hold mangroves up in soft mud and also take in some air, and some mangroves have snorkel-like roots called pneumatophores that stick up and take in air, because the mud has little oxygen.
fact: Mudskippers are fish that spend much of their time out of water; they keep water in their gill chambers and breathe through their skin, and males jump high to show off and defend their patch of mud.
fact: Archerfish shoot jets of water to knock insects into the water, and they aim correctly even though water bends light and makes things look in a different place.
fact: Male fiddler crabs wave one giant claw to attract females and warn rivals, and they eat with the small claw because the big one is too large.
fact: The Sundarbans of India and Bangladesh is the biggest mangrove forest in the world, and its Bengal tigers swim between islands and eat fish, crabs and deer.
fact: Saltwater crocodiles are the largest living reptiles, live in both salt and fresh water, swim far out to sea, and are the most dangerous crocodiles for people.
fact: Mangrove seeds sprout while still on the tree, drop as long spikes, and can float for months until they reach mud and grow roots.
fact: Mangrove roots break waves and hold the soil, and after the 2004 tsunami, places with mangroves often had less damage.
fact: Many young reef fish grow up safely among mangrove roots, so where mangroves are cut down, some reefs have fewer fish.
fact: Water monitors are big lizards that swim with their long tails, hunt crabs and fish, and can stay underwater for many minutes.
exercise: What is unusual about mangrove trees? | They grow in salty water along tropical coasts | They grow only high up on cold, snowy mountains | They grow without any roots | They grow only in deserts
exercise: How do some mangroves get rid of extra salt? | They push it out through their leaves | They store it in their fruit | They spit it into the air from their roots | They never take in any salt
exercise: Why do some mangroves have roots sticking up out of the mud? | The mud has little oxygen, so these roots take in air | To catch fish | To stop the little tree crabs from climbing up the trunk | To drink rainwater
exercise: What do stilt roots do for a mangrove? | Hold it up in soft mud and help it breathe | Let it walk slowly along the beach to a new place | Catch insects | Store honey
exercise: How can a mudskipper stay out of water? | It keeps water in its gill chambers and breathes through its skin | It has lungs like a dog | It holds its breath for days | It never leaves the water
exercise: Why do male mudskippers jump high? | To show off and defend their mud patch | To catch birds that fly too low over the mud | To dry their wet skin in the warm sunshine | To reach the treetops and look for food
exercise: How does an archerfish catch insects? | It shoots a jet of water to knock them into the water | It jumps out onto the leaf and bites them with its teeth | It sings to them | It waits for them to fall asleep
exercise: Why is an archerfish's aim so impressive? | Water bends light, so it must aim at a place that looks wrong | It shoots with its eyes closed tight | It aims backwards | It shoots only in the dark, when the insects cannot see it coming
exercise: What does a male fiddler crab use its giant claw for? | Waving to attract females and warn rivals | Eating all its food faster than the others | Digging deep for buried shells in the soft mud | Swimming fast to get away from birds and fish
exercise: Which is the biggest mangrove forest in the world? | The Sundarbans of India and Bangladesh | The Sahara | The Amazon rainforest in Brazil and Peru | The Great Barrier Reef
exercise: What do the Sundarbans tigers do that many tigers rarely do? | Swim between islands and eat fish and crabs | Climb high into the clouds | Live in snow caves on the mountains all winter | Eat only fruit
exercise: Which are the largest living reptiles? | Saltwater crocodiles | Komodo dragons from Indonesia | Big green sea turtles | Burmese pythons and anacondas
exercise: What should people do where saltwater crocodiles live? | Stay well away from riverbanks | Swim out to them to calm them down | Feed them by hand to keep them friendly | Stand on the bank to watch them swim
exercise: How do mangrove seeds travel? | They sprout on the tree, drop as spikes and float until they reach mud | Birds carry them in their beaks | The wind blows them into the sky | They stay under their mother tree forever
exercise: How do mangroves protect coasts from storms? | Their roots break the waves and hold the soil | They pull the clouds away | They suck up the sea | They make the wind blow the other way, back out to sea
exercise: Why are mangroves called a nursery for fish? | Young fish hide safely among the roots | Fish lay their eggs high up in the tree branches | Teachers bring fish there | The trees feed fish milk
exercise: How does a water monitor swim? | With its long tail | With its long tongue | With its claws only | It cannot swim at all
test: «mangroves r just trees that got flooded, they'll die soon» What is right? | No, they are made to live with their roots in salty water | Yes, every flood kills them | Yes, salt water kills all trees, even the strong ones | No, they are a kind of seaweed
test: «mudskipper walking on mud?? fish cant leave water mate» What is right? | Mudskippers keep water in their gills and breathe through their skin, so they can | Right, it must be a lizard | Right, it will die in a minute | It is a frog, not a fish
test: In Peter Pan the crocodile follows Captain Hook because it swallowed a ticking clock. What is true of real saltwater crocodiles? | They are dangerous, so people must stay far from riverbanks | They follow ticking sounds | They are friendly to pirates | They live only in cold mountain lakes, far away from the sea and from people
test: In The Little Mermaid, Sebastian the crab sings with two equal claws. How is a male fiddler crab different? | One claw is giant and one is small | It has no claws at all, only soft legs | It has four big claws, two on each side | Both claws are tiny and the same size
test: «2 fiddler crabs, each got 1 big claw n 1 small, how many small claws» | 2 | 4 | 1 | 3
test: «spotted 3 tigers swimming in the Sundarbans, how many legs n tails altogether» | 15 | 12 | 9 | 18
test: What would happen to a coast if all its mangroves were cut down? | Storm waves would hit harder, and some reefs would have fewer fish | The coast would be safer from storms, with no trees in the way of the sea | There would be more young fish | Nothing would change
test: An archerfish shoots at a beetle on a leaf above the water. Why does it not aim where the beetle seems to be? | Water bends light, so the beetle looks in the wrong place | It wants to miss on purpose | It cannot see the beetle at all from under the water | Water makes everything look bigger only
test: «crocs only live in rivers, the sea's too salty for em» What is right? | Saltwater crocodiles live in salt and fresh water and swim far out to sea | Right, salt kills crocodiles | Crocodiles live only in deserts | Only baby crocodiles go in the sea
test: A long green spike floats on the sea for months and lands on a muddy beach. What is it most likely? | A mangrove seedling that will grow roots | A lost pencil | A dead fish | A piece of the tail of a swimming tiger
test: «dad says there's a lizard swimming under the boat for ages, lizards can't do that» | Water monitors swim with their tails and stay under for many minutes | Right, all lizards sink | It must be a fish | Lizards breathe water through gills, like fish, so they never need to come up
exam: Identify the function of the pneumatophores of certain species of mangrove. | They protrude from the mud and admit air to the root system | They secrete salt into the surrounding sea | They capture insects for nutrition | They anchor the tree to rock
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hunting method of the archerfish? | It dislodges insects with a jet of water, correcting for the bending of light | It ascends trees to seize insects | It captures insects with an extended tongue | It awaits insects falling of their own accord into the water beneath the mangroves
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Sundarbans. | It is the largest mangrove forest in the world and harbours swimming Bengal tigers | It is a desert in which tigers are absent | It is a coral reef in the Pacific Ocean | It is a mountain forest devoid of water
exam: Ascertain the principal benefit conferred upon coastlines by mangrove forests. | Their roots diminish the force of waves and retain the soil | They prevent the formation of clouds | They raise the temperature of the sea | They repel all marine animals by means of a poison exuded by their roots
